From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 03:17:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] docs: document CallManager, the live-call singleton

Add KDoc explaining how CallManager mediates between Android's InCallService
and the in-call UI: it holds the active calls, tracks them via per-call
callbacks, derives a high-level PhoneState (getPhoneState), and exposes the call
controls. Comment-only.
---
 .../org/fossify/phone/helpers/CallManager.kt  | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/app/src/main/kotlin/org/fossify/phone/helpers/CallManager.kt b/app/src/main/kotlin/org/fossify/phone/helpers/CallManager.kt
index 51541c74..14b36f15 100644
--- a/app/src/main/kotlin/org/fossify/phone/helpers/CallManager.kt
+++ b/app/src/main/kotlin/org/fossify/phone/helpers/CallManager.kt
@@ -12,6 +12,18 @@ import org.fossify.phone.extensions.isConference
 import org.fossify.phone.models.AudioRoute
 import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArraySet
 
+/**
+ * The single source of truth for the phone's live call(s).
+ *
+ * Everything is in the `companion object`, i.e. it is effectively a process-wide
+ * singleton, because there is only ever one telephony session: Android's
+ * [InCallService] (see CallService) feeds call add/remove/audio events in here,
+ * and the in-call UI reads state and issues control actions (accept, reject,
+ * hold, swap, merge, keypad tones, audio routing) back out. It keeps the list of
+ * active calls, registers a per-call `Call.Callback` to stay current, derives a
+ * high-level [PhoneState] (no call / single / two calls / conference) via
+ * [getPhoneState], and fans changes out to registered [CallManagerListener]s.
+ */
 // inspired by https://github.com/Chooloo/call_manage
 class CallManager {
     companion object {
@@ -54,6 +66,13 @@ class CallManager {
             }
         }
 
+        /**
+         * Collapses the raw call list into a high-level [PhoneState] the UI can
+         * switch on: [NoCall], [SingleCall], [TwoCalls] (ordered so the more
+         * "foreground" call — the newly dialing/connecting one, else the active
+         * one — is primary), or a conference. With more than two calls it looks
+         * for the conference call and any lone second call outside it.
+         */
         fun getPhoneState(): PhoneState {
             return when (calls.size) {
                 0 -> NoCall

